 the bounding box assumption?\nL1840: \nL1841: –  【41†Dmitry Kamenetsky】\nL1842: \nL1843: Commented May 15, 2022 at 9:58\nL1844: \nL1845: 【34†Add a comment】  |\nL1846: \nL1847: [Input]\nL1848: \nL1849: 9\nL1850: \nL1851: [Input]\nL1852: \nL1853: In the interest of getting the process started, here's a quick (but not very optimal) answer.\nL1854: ### Pattern:\nL1855: \nL1856:     `n = 8:\nL1857:     ########\nL1858:      ######\nL1859:       #####\nL1860:        ####\nL1861:         ###\nL1862:          ##\nL1863:     `\nL1864: \nL1865: Take a triangle with length n - 1, stick an extra square onto the corner, and cut off the bottom square.\nL1866: ### Proof that all n-ominos fit:\nL1867: \nL1868: Note that any n-omino can fit in a rectangle with length + width at most n + 1.\nL1869: \nL1870: If an n-omino fits in a rectangle with length + width at most n, it fits nicely in the triangle (which is the union of all such rectangles). If it happens to use the cut-off square, transposing it will fit in the triangle.\nL1871: Otherwise, we have a chain with at most one branch. We can always fit one end of the chain into the extra square (prove this with casework), and the rest fits into a rectangle with length + width at most n, reducing to the case above.\nL1872: \nL1873: The only case where the above doesn't work is the case where we use both the extra square and the cut-off square. There's only one such n-omino (the long L), and that one fits inside the triangle transposed.\nL1874: ### Code (Python 2):\nL1875: \nL1876:     `def f(n):\nL1877:         if n < 7:\nL1878:             return [0, 1, 2, 4, 6, 9, 12][n]\nL1879:         return n * (n - 1) / 2\nL1880:     `\nL1881: ### Table:\nL1882: \nL1883:     ` 1:   1\nL1884:      2:   2\nL1885:      3:   4\nL1886:      4:   6\nL1887:      5:   9\nL1888:      6:  12\nL1889:      7:  21\nL1890:      8:  28\nL1891:      9:  36\nL1892:     10:  45\nL1893:     11:  55\nL1894:     12:  66\nL1895:     13:  78\nL1896:     14:  91\nL1897:     15: 105\nL1898:     16: 120\nL1899:     17: 136\nL1900:     18: 153\nL1901:     19: 171\nL1902:     20: 190\nL1903:     ... more cases can be generated if necessary.\nL1904:     `\nL1905: \nL1906: 【42†Improve this answer】\nL1907: \nL1908: Follow [Input]\nL1909: \nL1910: 【43†edited Jun 26, 2018 at 20:52】\nL1911: \nL1912: answered Jun 26, 2018 at 18:55\nL1913: \nL1914: user48543\nL1915:   * I think you can remove the bottom two squares for sufficiently large \\$n\\$. Mock proof: Aside from the two squares on the ends of the top row the shape has a mirror symmetry. Thus any shape that occupies the one of the bottom two either can be mirrored out of them or occupies the top right square. All the ones that occupy one of the bottom two and the top right, are within a 3x(n-1) footprint. We have a 3x(n-1) rectangle in the top 3 rows minus a single corner.\nL1916: The remaining shapes can only occupy a max of 2 corners in the bounding box, thus they can be rotated to fit in the top 3 rows.\nL1917: –  【44†Wheat Wizard】 ♦\nL1918: \nL1919: Commented Jun 26, 2018 at 22:59\nL1920: \nL1921:   * The character limit kind of got me. If you need more explanation I can clarify.\nL1922: \nL1923: –  【44†Wheat Wizard】 ♦\nL1924: \nL1925: Commented Jun 26, 2018 at 23:00\nL1926: \nL1927:   * @CatWizard I see what you mean. I wouldn't be surprised if you could extend it to removing about half the rows (for large n).\nL1928: \nL1929: –  user48543\nL1930: \nL1931: Commented Jun 26, 2018 at 23:29\nL1932: \nL1933: 【34†Add a comment】  |\nL1934: \nL1935: [Input]\nL1936: \nL1937: 8\nL1938: \nL1939: [Input]\nL2295:                 v = (v & 0x33333333) + ((v >> 2) & 0x33333333);\nL2296:                 return ((v + (v >> 4) & 0xF0F0F0F) * 0x1010101) >> 24;\nL2297:             }\nL2298: \nL2299:             public static IEnumerable<T> Shuffle<T>(this IEnumerable<T> elts, Random rnd = null)\nL2300:             {\nL2301:                 rnd = rnd ?? new Random();\nL2302:                 T[] arr = elts.ToArray();\nL2303:                 int n = arr.Length;\nL2304:                 while (n > 0)\nL2305:                 {\nL2306:                     int idx = rnd.Next(n - 1);\nL2307:                     yield return arr[idx];\nL2308:                     arr[idx] = arr[n - 1];\nL2309:                     arr[n - 1] = default(T); // Help GC if T is a class\nL2310:                     n--;\nL2311:                 }\nL2312:             }\nL2313:         }\nL2314:     }\nL2315:     `\nL2512:         for _ in 0..n-1 {\nL2513:             let mut next_polys = HashSet::new();\nL2514:             for poly in polys {\nL2515:                 next_polys.extend(poly.extend());\nL2516:             }\nL2517:             polys = next_polys;\nL2518:         }\nL2519:         polys\nL2520:     }\nL2521: \nL2522:     fn overlap_polys(polys: &Vec<Poly>, seed: u64) -> Poly {\nL2523:         let mut seq = polys.clone();\nL2524:         let mut seed_array = [0; 32];\nL2525:         for i in 0..32 {\nL2526:             seed_array[i] = seed.to_be().to_bytes()[i%8];\nL2527:         }\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncode challenge - Smallest region of the plane that contains all free n-ominoes - Code Golf Stack Exchange (https://codegolf.stackexchange.com/questions/167484/smallest-region-of-the-plane-that-contains-all-free-n-ominoes)\nciteturn4view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view0\",\"pattern\":\"11\"}); Total lines: 2664\nL179: ## 4 Answers\nL180: \nL181: Sorted by:\nL182: \nL183: [Select]\nL184: \nL185: [Input]\nL186: \nL187: 11\nL188: \nL189: [Input]\nL190: \nL191: +150\nL192: ## C# and SAT: 1, 2, 4, 6, 9, 12, 17, 20, 26, 31, 37, 43\nL193: \nL194: If we limit the bounding box, there is a fairly obvious expression of the problem in terms of 【35†SAT†en.wikipedia.org】: each translation of each orientation of each free polyomino is a large conjunction; for each polyomino we form a disjunction over its conjunctions; and then we require each disjunction to be true and the total number of cells used to be limited.\n"